meta {
  name: 1. Contact form (Turnstile) - success
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/v1/send
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Origin: https://acme.example
}

body:json {
  {
    "brand": "acme",
    "name": "Jane Smith",
    "email": "jane@example.com",
    "type": "Consultation",
    "message": "Please get in touch about your services.",
    "cf-turnstile-response": "test-token"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.success: eq true
}

docs {
  # Public contact form (Turnstile brand)

  The canonical browser-form submission. `auth: none` keeps this in **public
  mode**, which enforces the full gate stack: origin allowlist, honeypot,
  Turnstile, rate limit, and a fixed recipient (`brand.to`). Any `to`/`from` in
  the body would be ignored (anti-relay); this brand always mails
  `inbox@acme.example`.

  `acme` uses Cloudflare Turnstile with action `contact`. Replace `test-token`
  with a fresh widget token from an allowed origin and configure its matching
  secret. Siteverify must return an allowed hostname and the configured action;
  an always-pass testing secret alone does not satisfy those checks.

  Requires a working SES path (real or mocked) for the send to succeed and
  return 200 — otherwise the handler returns 502 `send failed`. Because `acme`
  has `autoReply: true`, a "we received your message" confirmation is also sent
  to the submitter, best-effort via `ctx.waitUntil` (see the opt-out request).
}
